Notice that the write and read functions now return a type called AsyncStatus instead of just a simple Result. This AsyncStatus type can represent either a file descriptor or a timeout, depending on the specific operation being performed. Using this AsyncStatus type, we can now perform asynchronous I/O operations.

To see this in action, let's imagine writing some code that asynchronously reads from a file:

let mut file = File :: open ( "my_file.txt" ). unwrap ();
let mut buffer = [ 0 ; CHUNK_SIZE ];
loop {
    match file . async_read ( & mut buffer ) {
        Ok ( AsyncStatus :: Fd ( _ )) => {
            // We got some data from the file
            // Do something with the data
        },
        Ok ( AsyncStatus :: Timeout ( _ )) => {
            // We timed out before getting any data
            // Do something else
        },
        Err ( e ) => {
            // There was an error
            if e . kind () == ErrorKind :: WouldBlock {
                // This just means we need to wait for more data
                continue ;
            } else {
                // Some other kind of error occurred
                panic !( "Error reading file: {}" , e );
            }
        }
    }
}

This code uses the async_read function to read data from a file. If the read operation succeeds, it returns an AsyncStatus::Fd value indicating that we got some data. If the read operation times out, it returns an AsyncStatus::Timeout value. If some other kind of error occurs, it simply panics.

Your first Firefox (Web)extension in KotlinKotlin, my favourite programming language, has seen a fast adoption on platforms like Android or Server where the code is compiled to Java bytecode. However, as Kotlin also supports compilation to Javascript, the language is starting to receive attention in the Web ecosystem as well.While some content on writing web applications in Kotlin has been published, the niche of writing browser extensions has seemed to be ignored until now. That's why in this post we are going explore the process of writing a simple extension for Firefox using Kotlin JS.The extension is based on Your first extension by Mozilla and will simply add a red border to the kotlinlang.org website.RequirementsFor testing our Firefox extension, we will use the web-ext tool from Mozilla. This command-line tool makes it easy to develop, test, and debug extensions for Firefox. To use web-ext, ensure that you have Node.js and npm installed.

To get started, create a new directory for your extension and navigate to it in your terminal. Then, install the web-ext tool by running:

npm install --global web-ext

Next, create a new Kotlin JS project using your preferred build tool, such as Gradle or Maven. You'll need to include the kotlin-stdlib-js library as a dependency so that you can use the language to write client-side scripts.

Once your project is set up, create a new JavaScript file called background.js in your extension's root directory. This script will run in the background and listen for events from the browser. Here's an example implementation:

```
browser.tabs.onUpdated.addListener((tabId, changeInfo, tab) => {
  if (changeInfo.status === 'complete' && tab.url.includes('kotlinlang.org')) {
    browser.tabs.executeScript(tabId, {file: 'content.js'});
  }
});
```

This script listens for updates to the browser tabs and checks if the updated page is the kotlinlang.org website. If it is, the script injects a content script (which we'll create next) into the tab.

Create a new JavaScript file called content.js in your extension's root directory. This script will run in the context of the web page and modify its contents. Here's an example implementation:

```
document.body.style.border = '5px solid red';
```

This script simply adds a red border to the top-level body element of the page.

To test your extension, run the following command in your extension's root directory:

web-ext run

This will launch a new instance of Firefox with your extension installed. Navigate to the kotlinlang.org website and you should see the red border around the page.

In conclusion, writing browser extensions in Kotlin JS is a great way to leverage your existing skills and tools to build powerful and customizable browser add-ons. With the help of web-ext, testing and debugging your extensions is a breeze.
